## Progress protocol

For each acceptance criterion n, print an isolated line `BATUTA-PROGRESS <n> START` before the first edit toward it and `BATUTA-PROGRESS <n> DONE` when its proof passes locally. Same shape as the `BATUTA-QUESTION:` line: plain text on stdout, nothing else on that line, no tool required. Criterion numbers are the 1-based positions of the `## Acceptance criteria` list.

## Boundaries

Do not touch CI configuration, licenses, lockfiles (except through an allowed dependency), `WORK.md` or anything under `.batuta/`. Do not push, do not merge, do not change branches.

## Scope

- `loop/brief.go`
- `loop/testdata/**`

Do not change anything outside this list; if the task requires it, stop and report.

## Expected evidence

In your final message: the paths you touched, each command you ran with its real output, and anything you did not verify, declared as such. Never claim a result you did not observe.

## Stop conditions

Stop and report instead of improvising when: (1) the code's shape contradicts this brief; (2) the same command fails twice; (3) the fix needs edits beyond Scope or Boundaries. To ask the human a question, print exactly one final line `BATUTA-QUESTION: <your question>` and exit 0; the run pauses and resumes in this worktree with the answer.

## Test laws

1. Test the behavior, never the mock.
2. A failing test means fix the code, not the test.
3. No test-only flags or branches in production code.

## Method

Work test-first from the acceptance criteria. Investigate root cause before fixing a bug; never silence a signal (cast, suppression, empty catch, sleep) instead of fixing its source — if you must, mark `// WORKAROUND: <reason>` and say so in your report.
